{"name":"Pearl Ash","hex":"#d0c9c3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pearl ash","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of pearlash."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pearl_ash"}],"entries":[{"title":"Potash","blocks":[{"text":"Potash is a name for mined and manufactured salts that contain potassium in water-soluble form."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/PotashUSGOV.jpg/330px-PotashUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potash","wikidata":"Q10564271"},{"title":"Pearl","blocks":[{"text":"A pearl is a hard, shiny object produced within the soft tissue of a living shelled mollusk. Like the shell of a mollusk, a pearl is composed of calcium carbonate in a crystalline form, which is deposited in concentric layers. More commercially valuable pearls are perfectly round and smooth, but many other shapes, known as baroque pearls, can occur. The highest quality of natural pearls have been highly valued as gemstones and objects of beauty for many centuries. In literature, pearls have become a metaphor for something rare, fine, admirable, and valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/330px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl","wikidata":"Q43436"},{"title":"Ash","blocks":[{"text":"Ash is the solid remnants of fires. Specifically, ash refers to all non-aqueous, non-gaseous residues that remain after something burns. In analytical chemistry, to analyse the mineral and metal content of chemical samples, ash is the non-gaseous, non-liquid residue after complete combustion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/330px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ash","wikidata":"Q152079"},{"title":"Pearl (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color pearl is a pale tint of off-white. It is a representation of the average color of a pearl."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/PerlmuttAusst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_(color)","wikidata":"Q7158021"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg/500px-First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg/1280px-First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg/500px-First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The very first U.S. patent ever to be  issued was for an improvement \"in the making of Pot ash and Pearl ash by a new Apparatus and Process\"; it was signed by then President George Washington.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFirst_United_States_of_America_patent_(July_31%2C_1790).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg/500px-CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg/1280px-CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb